  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1202 AM MST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nModels in good agreement through the extended period, with an\nupper storm system mid week with increasing chances for\nprecipitation.\n\nSunday through Tuesday...broad westerly zonal flow will prevail\nthrough the early part of the week. Temperatures will be cool on\nSunday, and gusty north winds will continue near the Kansas\nborder. Highs are forecast to reach the mid 40s across the\nregion. Temperatures quickly warm Monday into Tuesday as\nwesterly flow increases and enhances mixing. Winds will warm\nback through the 50s to lower 60s both Monday and Tuesday. Winds\nwill remain elevated in gap wind prone areas along the I-25\ncorridor, with gusts 25 to 35 mph possible. Humidity values are\nforecast to remain above 15 percent.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...models in good agreement this cycle\nwith the evolution and track of an upper storm system mid week.\nAll guidance tracks an upper low off the southern California\nCoast and across the Desert Southwest. This storm track is very\nfavorable for precipitation across southern Colorado. The main\nissue may be temperatures. Given the origin of the upper system,\nit appears on the warmer side. Mountain areas will see snowfall,\nespecially for the San Juan and southern Sangre de Cristo\nranges. For areas on the Plains, deep upslope flow wrapping\naround the system and an upper trowal development along the I-25\ncorridor by Wednesday night into Thursday morning. Again, snow\nprogs are not overly high on the Plains given the expected QPF\nforecast. If colder air can coincide with the heavier\nprecipitation, decent snowfall amounts could be possible. There\nis still a lot of uncertainty at this time, but model trends, AI\nguidance and ensembles all continue to increase QPF amounts. But\nit does look promising for southern Colorado to see\nprecipitation.\n\nFriday and Saturday...as the upper system departs, upper ridging\nwill fill in behind on Friday into Saturday. Dry conditions and\na slight warming trend are likely.",
